<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Deliberately empty.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  WHY A BLANK ICON RATHER THAN NO ICON
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Android 12 and later ALWAYS draws a system splash window. There is no
  attribute, manifest flag or library call that turns it off; the platform
  owns that window and the app only gets to say what is in it. An app that
  sets no windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon gets the launcher icon by default,
  which is the tile appearing before the real launch screen.

  So the icon slot is filled with nothing. A vector with a single fully
  transparent path is valid, costs one drawable, and paints no pixels, so
  the system splash is windowSplashScreenBackground and nothing else: a
  flat amethyst screen that hands over to SplashScreen.jsx on the same
  colour. The join is invisible because both sides are the same flat fill.

  The four point spark that used to be here is still drawn everywhere else
  the brand uses it (BrandSeal, the tab bar, both app bars); it is only
  absent from this one window, because the owner wants the launch artwork
  to be the first thing anybody sees.

  ── The PWA is a different story ────────────────────────────────────
  None of this reaches an installed PWA. Chrome generates that launch
  screen from the web manifest's background_color, name and 512px icon,
  and there is no way to suppress it either. Matching the colours is the
  whole of the available fix there. See vite.config.js.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="288dp"
    android:height="288dp"
    android:viewportWidth="288"
    android:viewportHeight="288">
    <path
        android:pathData="M0,0h288v288h-288z"
        android:fillColor="#00000000" />
</vector>
